# Test: priority-decrement tracking reaches the wire. An operator who writes
# `track interface <name> priority-decrement <n>` gets an advertised priority
# that falls by n while that interface is down, and rises again when it returns.
#
# VALIDATES:
#   AC-6 -- a non-owner group at priority 200 tracking zetrk1 with a decrement
#   of 150 advertises 50 once zetrk1 goes down. The proof is the PRIORITY BYTE
#   of the advertisement, captured off the parent's veth peer: that byte is what
#   a neighboring router elects on, so it is the only observation that shows
#   the decrement reached the wire rather than only the configuration.
#   ADVERT-PRIORITY 200 tracked-interface-up establishes the undecremented
#   baseline first, so the 50 that follows is a CHANGE rather than a value that
#   might always have been there.
#
#   AC-7 -- zetrk1 comes back up and the group advertises 200 again. A one-way
#   decrement would leave a healthy router permanently unable to win an
#   election, so the recovery leg is not optional.
#
#   RFC 9568 Section 5.2.4 -- "VRRP Routers backing up a Virtual Router MUST use
#   priority values between 1-254 (decimal)." Both observed values sit in that
#   range, and neither is 0 (which the same section reserves for the Active
#   Router that stopped participating) nor 255 (the address owner's).
#
# PREVENTS: a decrement that reaches GroupSpec and never the advertisement,
#   which is a failover an operator configured and Ze silently never performs;
#   and a decrement that is never withdrawn, which is the same failure with the
#   router stuck on the losing side of it.
#
# The tracked interface carries no address and no relationship to the group's
# family: Ze reads its operational state ONLY. That is the point of tracking a
# separate veth rather than the parent.
#
# option=needs-linux: the proof needs veth creation, macvlan creation, a raw
# proto-112 socket and an AF_PACKET capture. None of them exists on darwin.
# Runs as root in the QEMU Linux VM.
# caps=net-admin because the setup fixture creates the two veth pairs, the
# driver takes one down and up again, and the daemon programs netlink. Without
# the declaration an unprivileged Linux host does not skip: the calls are
# refused and the test fails for a capability the host never had
# (internal/test/runner/record_parse.go, option=needs-linux).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
# option=exclusive: reconcileOwnedDevices (iface/config_apply.go) deletes every
# "ze:owned:" macvlan absent from ITS registry, so two ze daemons owning
# macvlans in one network namespace remove each other's devices. The group is
# named for the resource rather than for vrrp: any plugin registering an owned
# macvlan contends for the same alias namespace.
option=exclusive:group=iface-owned-macvlan


stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
interface {
	backend netlink;
	ethernet zeptrk0 {
		unit 0 {
			ipv4 {
				address [ 203.0.113.251/24 ];
				vrrp {
					group uplink {
						vrid 12;
						virtual-address [ 203.0.113.1 ];
						priority 200;
						advertise-interval-milliseconds 1000;
						track {
							interface zetrk1 {
								priority-decrement 150;
							}
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.vrrp:value=info

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture vrrp/vrrp-track-setup
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze-test fixture vrrp/vrrp-track-driver:timeout=180s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=ADVERT-PRIORITY 200 tracked-interface-up
expect=stdout:contains=ADVERT-PRIORITY 50 tracked-interface-down
expect=stdout:contains=ADVERT-PRIORITY 200 tracked-interface-restored
expect=stderr:contains=vrrp: tracked interface state changed
